In C, N=4096 and a is a rectangular array of eight-byte doubles. Which loop has the contiguous inner access pattern and fewer demand misses in the lesson's cold aligned 64-byte-line, 32 KiB fully associative LRU model, with no prefetching or other accesses?

```
// A
for (i = 0; i < N; i++)
  for (j = 0; j < N; j++)
    s += a[i][j];
// B
for (j = 0; j < N; j++)
  for (i = 0; i < N; i++)
    s += a[i][j];
```

Assume 64-byte lines, 8-byte doubles and N large. How many cache misses per inner-loop iteration does this have?

```
for (i = 0; i < N; i++)
  for (j = 0; j < N; j++)
    s += a[j][i] * b[i][j];
``` Use the lesson's simplified demand-allocation model: aligned arrays, no prefetching/conflicts/interference, no whole-row/column retention between sweeps, current-line spatial reuse, and fixed scalars retained in registers. Count model misses, not elapsed time.
